From: gts9wifi port <gts9wifi@localhost>
Subject: avcodec/v4l2_buffers: pass timestamps through when no time base is set

v4l2_get_timebase() took avctx->pkt_timebase if its numerator was set and
avctx->time_base otherwise.  A decoder whose caller sets neither (Firefox:
it hands libavcodec packets with pts in microseconds and leaves both at
their defaults, time_base = 0/1) then rescaled every input pts with 0/1
(every OUTPUT buffer queued at timestamp 0) and every output timestamp with
a zero denominator (every frame returned as AV_NOPTS_VALUE).  Firefox drops
the hardware decoder after the first frame when that happens and falls back
to software.

Use V4L2's own time base (1/1000000) when neither is valid, so timestamps
go through the device unchanged; a valid pkt_timebase or time_base is
still used as before.  Check the denominator too.

Forwarded: not yet
Last-Update: 2026-10-27
---
--- a/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
+++ b/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
@@ -52,9 +52,15 @@
 {
     V4L2m2mContext *s = buf_to_m2mctx(avbuf);
 
-    if (s->avctx->pkt_timebase.num)
+    if (s->avctx->pkt_timebase.num && s->avctx->pkt_timebase.den)
         return s->avctx->pkt_timebase;
-    return s->avctx->time_base;
+    if (s->avctx->time_base.num && s->avctx->time_base.den)
+        return s->avctx->time_base;
+    /* Neither is set (a decoder's time_base defaults to 0/1): pass the
+     * timestamps through unchanged instead of rescaling to and from 0/1,
+     * which sent every packet at 0 and returned every frame as
+     * AV_NOPTS_VALUE. */
+    return v4l2_timebase;
 }
 
 static inline void v4l2_set_pts(V4L2Buffer *out, int64_t pts)
